[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Error Handling, TRY/CATCH, and XACT_STATE in Microsoft SQL Server"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Robust error handling is essential for maintaining database integrity, preventing partial data modifications, and providing meaningful diagnostic information. In Microsoft SQL Server, structured exception handling is achieved using TRY...CATCH blocks combined with the XACT_STATE() function to manage active transactions safely."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding TRY...CATCH in T-SQL"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The TRY...CATCH construct intercepts errors during execution. Statements are placed inside the BEGIN TRY block. If any error occurs, execution immediately jumps to the BEGIN CATCH block. Note that compile-time errors, such as syntax errors, are not caught."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"BEGIN TRY
    -- Operations that might fail
    SELECT 1 / 0 AS Result;
END TRY
BEGIN CATCH
    SELECT 
        ERROR_NUMBER() AS ErrorNumber,
        ERROR_MESSAGE() AS ErrorMessage;
END CATCH;"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Managing Transactions with XACT_STATE()"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When writing stored procedures that modify data, transactions are often utilized. If an error occurs inside a transaction, SQL Server can enter an uncommittable state. The XACT_STATE() function indicates the current transaction state and returns one of three values:"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"1: The current request has an active transaction that can be committed or rolled back."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"-1: The current request has an uncommittable transaction. The transaction must be rolled back."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"0: There is no active transaction for the current request."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Complete Production Pattern"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following template demonstrates a bulletproof pattern for handling errors and managing transactions safely using XACT_STATE()."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"CREATE PROCEDURE usp_SafeTransactionExample
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    BEGIN TRY
        BEGIN TRANSACTION;

        -- Perform data modifications here
        INSERT INTO dbo.AuditLog (LogMessage) VALUES ('Transaction started');

        -- Intentional error for demonstration
        RAISERROR('A critical error occurred.', 16, 1);

        COMMIT TRANSACTION;
    END TRY
    BEGIN CATCH
        -- Check if transaction is active or uncommittable
        IF XACT_STATE() <> 0
        begin
            ROLLBACK TRANSACTION;
        end

        -- Capture error details
        DECLARE @ErrorMessage NVARCHAR(4000) = ERROR_MESSAGE();
        DECLARE @ErrorSeverity INT = ERROR_SEVERITY();
        DECLARE @ErrorState INT = ERROR_STATE();

        RAISERROR (@ErrorMessage, @ErrorSeverity, @ErrorState);
    END CATCH;
END;"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use XACT_STATE() instead of @@TRANCOUNT in CATCH blocks because XACT_STATE specifically identifies whether a transaction can be safely committed."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include SET NOCOUNT ON at the beginning of procedures to prevent unwanted message traffic that can interfere with application logic."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use error logging inside the CATCH block to record details using ERROR_NUMBER(), ERROR_LINE(), and ERROR_PROCEDURE()."}]
